Description: universal undo for destructive shell commands
 rewind sits between your shell and commands that destroy state. It stages
 the pre-action state of rm, mv, cp, chmod, chown, git reset --hard,
 git clean, git checkout, and git restore, runs the real command with
 unchanged output and exit code, and keeps a local journal so the action
 can be reversed later.
 .
 Undo is resumable and works across commands: one "rewind undo" reverses
 the most recent destructive operation, and "rewind undo N" reverses the
 last N. A single binary provides the journal, trash, retention,
 and configuration commands; bash, zsh, and fish hooks make the wrapping
 invisible.
